 5.84, 5.85, 6.05, 6.07, 6.02, 6.32, 6.27, 6.52, 6.81 (next 12)\n# Then 6.86, 6.44, 6.39, 6.53, 6.94, nan... (last 12)\n\n# These are increasing - likely 1963, 1964, 1965...\n\n# Let me figure out the year mapping based on yield levels:\n# 1960: ~4%\n# 1961: ~4%\n# 1962: ~4%  \n# 1963: ~4%\n# 1964: ~4%\n# 1965: ~4-5%\n# 1966: ~5%\n# 1967: ~5-6%\n# 1968: ~6%\n# 1969: ~6-7%\n\n# Looking at col groups:\n# Col 0: ~3.9-4.3% -> 1960\n# Col 3: ~3.9-4.2% -> 1961\n# Col 6: ~4.1-4.7% -> 1962\n# Col 9: ~5.2-6.9% -> This spans multiple years\n\n# Wait! I think I've been misunderstanding the table layout.\n# Looking at the raw output more carefully:\n\n# The table might be showing:\n# - First 12 rows (Jan-Dec): All 4 years shown side by side\n# - Next 12 rows: More years\n# - etc.\n\n# Let me print all data in a cleaner format\nprint(\"Treasury yields by column group (first 3 months of each):\")\nfor col in [0, 3, 6, 9]:\n    print(f\"Col {col}: {all_data[0][col]}, {all_data[1][col]}, {all_data[2][col]}\")\n\n# And now Aa corporate:\nprint(\"\\nAa corporate yields by column group:\")\nfor col in [1, 4, 7, 10]:\n    print(f\"Col {col}: {all_data[0][col]}, {all_data[1][col]}, {all_data[2][col]}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
